  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nAn upper-level shortwave continues to move from southwest to\nnortheast across the region today which has been the focus for\nthe widespread rain. An area of scattered thunderstorms also\ncontinues to slowly move across portions of the James River\nValley and vicinity. While updrafts have struggled to become\noverly tall and wide with only around 500 J/kg of MUCAPE, deep \nlayer shear has increased to around 50 knots or so in this\narea. Thus, lightning activity has become fairly \nfrequent/widespread and a few individual cells have pulsed up to\nstrong levels on occasion. Some small hail (possibly up to the \nsize of nickels) will remain possible until storms move out \nby mid-afternoon. Highs this afternoon will be seasonably cold,\nonly ranging from the lower 40s northeast to the low to mid 50s\nsoutheast.\n\nRain will gradually diminish from southwest to northeast \nthrough the rest of today as we become more zonal aloft. \nHowever, abundant clouds should stick around through the night \nwhich may limit how cold we dip tonight. The NBM continues to \nadvertise a few below freezing values for lows tonight across \nthe northwest and north central but this may be a little bit \naggressive considering the overcast conditions. For now, will \nhold off on any freeze headlines. After a cool day Tuesday with\nhighs in the upper 40s to mid 50s, we may have a better shot to\nsee some more widespread subfreezing temperatures Tuesday night\nas clouds will be on the decrease. Freeze headlines may not be \nout of the question Tuesday night into Wednesday morning.\n\nHigh pressure and progressive ridging settles in Wednesday as\nthe pattern favors split flow with a closed low over the Four\nCorners region. This pattern will mean slightly warmer \ntemperatures with NBM highs forecast to mainly be in the mid 50s\nto lower 60s. Cluster analysis then suggests that there is a \nhigh likelihood that the southwest cutoff low will kick out and \nphase with the northern stream towards the end of the week, but \nwell east of our forecast area. Therefore, confidence is \nincreasing that precipitation chances will mainly remain east, \nleaving most of North Dakota dry through the end of the week and\ninto the weekend.\n\nA dry cold front will move through the region on Thursday with\nstrong high pressure on it's heels. Behind the front, models\nsuggest some strong low-level cold air advection and pressure\nrises along with very steep low-level lapse rates. The latest\nNAM and GFS soundings suggest much of western and central North\nDakota will see winds at the top of the mixed layer in the 40 to\n50 knot range Thursday afternoon so it is looking more and more\nlike a windy day with a potential need for eventual headlines.\nDepending on how much we dry out behind the front, there could\nbe some fire weather concerns southwest as mentioned in the\nprevious discussion. NBM guidance then suggests a warmup into \nthe weekend with some highs back in the 70s possible by\nSaturday and Sunday.",
